<Guide
  slug="what-to-bring-on-test-day"
  description="What to bring to your Life in the UK test, when to arrive and what happens at the test centre."
>
  <p class="note">
    ID rules have changed in recent years and may change again. Check the current list on
    <a href="https://www.gov.uk/life-in-the-uk-test" rel="noopener">GOV.UK</a> before your test day.
  </p>

  <h2>Bring the ID you booked with</h2>
  <p>
    You must bring the same identification you used when you booked your test. If your ID doesn't match your booking,
    you may not be allowed to sit the test, and you may lose your fee.
  </p>

  <h2>Arrive early</h2>
  <p>
    Plan your journey and arrive in good time. Test centres can refuse entry if you are late, and you would then need
    to book and pay again.
  </p>

  <h2>At the test centre</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your identity is checked before you go in.</li>
    <li>You can't take your phone, notes or bags into the test room.</li>
    <li>You answer 24 questions on a computer, with 45 minutes in total.</li>
    <li>You get your result before you leave.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The night before</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Do a short review rather than a long cram: sleep helps you remember.</li>
    <li>Look over the facts you got wrong most often.</li>
    <li>Get your ID and booking confirmation ready.</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>
